{
  "dom_id": "step-q345198",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14255499/assets/f154d0638a1fd0d1e4d5f56d1ee374c3c01cb31044b7c62e0205538ea51452a4.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with no lid using $12\\pi \\,{cm}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the largest possible volume ${V}_{\\text{max}}$ of the cylinder? Do not round the answer.\n\n${V}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $12\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}=12\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =12\\pi -\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{6}{r}-\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}(\\frac{6}{r}-\\frac{r}{2}) \\\\ V(r) & =(6r-\\frac{{r}^{3}}{2})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(6-\\frac{3{r}^{2}}{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (6-\\frac{3{r}^{2}}{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{3{r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =4 \\\\ r & =2\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=2$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=\\frac{-6\\pi r}{2}=-3\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(2)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nSo the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nFinally, to find the largest volume ${V}_{\\text{max}},$ we substitute $r=2\\,\\text{cm}$ into the equation for the volume $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{max}}=V(2) & =(6(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{3})\\pi  \\\\  & =(12-4)\\pi  \\\\  & =8\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
